            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"A true or spiritual believer whose heart has been inwardly transformed by God, representing the spiritual people of God (often associated with the Church) as opposed to merely outward or ethnic descent.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Romans 2:28-29\",\"Revelation 2:9\",\"Revelation 3:9\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"The literal, ethnic descendants of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, representing God's enduring covenantal promises and earthly people (emphasized in Dispensationalism which rejects spiritualizing the term).\",\"basis\":[\"Romans 9:1-5\",\"Romans 11:1-2\"],\"prominence\":\"dominant\"},{\"meaning\":\"The religious establishment or the unbelieving world in direct opposition to Jesus, primarily interpreted as a typological or symbolic reading of 'hoi Ioudaioi' in Johannine literature.\",\"basis\":[\"John 8:31-44\",\"John 11:47-53\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The symbolic interpretation of 'Jew' is heavily debated and largely depends on one's theological framework. Covenant theology frequently interprets the 'true Jew' symbolically as the Church or inward believers. Dispensationalism strongly maintains a literal, ethnic definition, rejecting the spiritualized substitution. Furthermore, Johannine usage is complex; identifying individuals or groups as 'most harmful' opponents based on this text has historically occurred, but such assessments are subjective, depend on diverse perspectives, and modern scholarship often restricts the term to specific first-century religious authorities rather than a universal symbol.\"}",
